{"name":"Gateway Arch","hex":"#88796c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gateway","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A passage that can be closed by use of a gate.","A place regarded as giving access to somewhere."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make available using a gateway, or access point."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gateway"},{"term":"arch","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An inverted U shape.","An arch-shaped arrangement of trapezoidal stones, designed to redistribute downward force outward."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form into an arch shape.","To cover with an arch or arches."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/arch"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gateway Arch","blocks":[{"text":"The Gateway Arch is a 630-foot-tall (192 m) monument in St. Louis, Missouri, United States. Clad in stainless steel and built in the form of a weighted catenary arch, it is the world's tallest arch and Missouri's tallest accessible structure. Some sources consider it the tallest human-made monument in the Western Hemisphere. Built as a monument to the westward expansion of the United States and officially dedicated to \"the American people\", the Arch, commonly referred to as \"The Gateway to the West\", is a National Historic Landmark in Gateway Arch National Park and has become a popular tourist destination, as well as an internationally recognized symbol of St. Louis."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/St_Louis_night_expblend_cropped.jpg/330px-St_Louis_night_expblend_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gateway_Arch","wikidata":"Q2027162"},{"title":"Arch","blocks":[{"text":"An arch is a curved vertical structure spanning an open space underneath it. Arches may support the load above them, or they may perform a purely decorative role. As a decorative element, the arch dates back to the 4th millennium BC, but structural load-bearing arches became popular only after their adoption by the Ancient Romans in the 4th century BC."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/St_Louis_Gateway_Arch.jpg/330px-St_Louis_Gateway_Arch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arch","wikidata":"Q12277"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/St._Louis_riverfront_after_demolition_for_Gateway_Arch_%281942%29.jpg/500px-St._Louis_riverfront_after_demolition_for_Gateway_Arch_%281942%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/St._Louis_riverfront_after_demolition_for_Gateway_Arch_%281942%29.jpg/1280px-St._Louis_riverfront_after_demolition_for_Gateway_Arch_%281942%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/St._Louis_riverfront_after_demolition_for_Gateway_Arch_%281942%29.jpg/500px-St._Louis_riverfront_after_demolition_for_Gateway_Arch_%281942%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The St. Louis riverfront after demolition","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASt._Louis_riverfront_after_demolition_for_Gateway_Arch_(1942).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/4/4d/Eero_Saarinen_with_Gateway_Arch_Model.jpg/250px-Eero_Saarinen_with_Gateway_Arch_Model.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/4d/Eero_Saarinen_with_Gateway_Arch_Model.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/4/4d/Eero_Saarinen_with_Gateway_Arch_Model.jpg/250px-Eero_Saarinen_with_Gateway_Arch_Model.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia